The `TopBytesRefAggregatorFunction` and `TopBytesRefAggregatorFunctionSupplier` classes are generated and should not be manually edited as per their comments. However, they seem to have been modified in this pull request. Ensure that these changes are necessary and reflect the intended modifications without altering the auto-generated code structure.
In the `addRawInput` method, there is a repeated pattern of checking if the `mask` is constant and handling it accordingly. This could be refactored into a separate method to avoid code duplication:
```java
private void addRawInputWithMask(Page page, BooleanVector mask) {
    BytesRefBlock block = page.getBlock(channels.get(0));
    BytesRefVector vector = block.asVector();
    if (vector != null) {
        addRawVector(vector, mask);
    } else {
        addRawBlock(block, mask);
    }
}
```
Then, the `addRawInput` method can be simplified:
```java
@Override
public void addRawInput(Page page, BooleanVector mask) {
    if (mask.isConstant()) {
        if (!mask.getBoolean(0)) {
            // Entire page masked away
            return;
        }
        // No masking
        BytesRefBlock block = page.getBlock(channels.get(0));
        BytesRefVector vector = block.asVector();
        if (vector != null) {
            addRawVector(vector);
        } else {
            addRawBlock(block);
        }
    } else {
        // Some positions masked away, others kept
        addRawInputWithMask(page, mask);
    }
}
```
In the `addRawBlock` and `addRawBlock(BytesRefBlock block, BooleanVector mask)` methods, there is a repeated pattern of iterating over the block's positions. This could also be refactored into a separate method to avoid code duplication:
```java
private void processBlockPositions(BytesRefBlock block, BooleanVector mask) {
    BytesRef scratch = new BytesRef();
    for (int p = 0; p < block.getPositionCount(); p++) {
        if (mask != null && !mask.getBoolean(p)) {
            continue;
        }
        if (block.isNull(p)) {
            continue;
        }
        int start = block.getFirstValueIndex(p);
        int end = start + block.getValueCount(p);
        for (int i = start; i < end; i++) {
            TopBytesRefAggregator.combine(state, block.getBytesRef(i, scratch));
        }
    }
}
```
Then, the `addRawBlock` methods can be simplified:
```java
private void addRawBlock(BytesRefBlock block) {
    processBlockPositions(block, null);
}
private void addRawBlock(BytesRefBlock block, BooleanVector mask) {
    processBlockPositions(block, mask);
}
```
